ussia  is not able to  use  proceeds from its sales of oil  in the global markets to finance the war  and so  very naturally  europe the united states has now banned  imports of russian oil um we've had an  announcement this morning from president  fonder lion that uh the european union  is looking at  cutting off russian oil imports over a  span of the rest of this year  um we need to see the conditions um  exactly how this is going to be  accomplished because it could lead to  higher higher global oil prices as well  but the desire to  to respond to russia's unprovoked attack  on ukraine and to bring the war to an  end is very understandable and important  yeah i was going to ask if uh  morally ethically  the eu action  uh  it obviously comes to that place  economically is it wise is it worrisome  to you  um so you know i'm not sure that i  really understand the details of how  this is going to be accomplished um i  think it's very important that europe is  going to  rid itself of um  dependence that it has on russia as a  source of  supply and we we clearly want to work  with europe to make sure that they have  the energy supplies that are needed um i  think we need to do what we can over the  next year to make sure that we limit the  revenue that russia is able to  get  from selling oil in global markets and  also do what we can to make sure that  we don't see  a large escalation in global oil prices  um a couple of other aspects  of the economy right now you mentioned  the the signs of slowdown in china and  europe  now you're known i think is a bit of a  dollar hawk  the dollar has  grown quite a bit this year it surged  quite quickly are you concerned  that the dollar is getting too strong  and what could we do if so to mitigate  that  look i believe in a  market-based value for the dollar  when  countries are taking actions  designed to purposely  push their currencies down to gain an  unfair trade advantage vis-a-vis the  united states  the united states is prepared to respond  strongly to  um  purposeful um attempts to  uh of other countries to devalue their  currencies but believe in a market-based  value for the dollar  you know clearly u.s interest rates are  rising have risen relative to those in  the le in the rest of the world and so  one of the factors that's  at work i think pushing up the dollar is  um a redirection of  um investments toward  higher yielding  uh returns in the united states and the  in a way that's part of how a tighter  monetary policy works  i want to ask you two other kind of  broader questions about the economy  right now  one is moments like this when things are  shifting quickly when markets are in  some flux as ours have been and coming  down  it's moments like this where you  sometimes tend to see problems come out  uh you know you were you were actually  an economist who early uh nearly 20  years ago uh was warning about the  subprime mortgage problem before that  manifested  at that time so  as you look across the economy and think  where could potential problems lie where  could  things be lurking that that we haven't  seen below the surface are there  particular  areas of concern or interest that you're  looking at closely  uh so  banks are  quite strong i think that the  um reforms that were put in place  uh by dodd-frank after the financial  crisis have led to  more capital  more liquidity and better risk  management  at in the core of our banking system  banks who've endured a lot of stress and  have been able to meet the credit needs  of the economy that's sometimes a sector  where we have concerns  um  we  did see  when the pandemic struck stresses  if it showed up in  in non-bank  the non-bank financial sector in money  market funds  open end  mutual funds often bond funds  perhaps in the hedge fund space  and certainly stresses in markets that  cause the fed to  intervene to stabilize things in a  really almost unprecedented  fashion and um the financial stability  oversight council working with the sec  we're working to address weaknesses in  those sectors to make it less crisis  prone  so  i i feel good about  the strength the overall strength of the  financial sector and its resilience  asset valuations  uh the fed is noted in its recent  reports in in the f-soc uh annual report  we noted that asset valuations are high  in housing obviously there's been a huge  increase in housing prices  ratio of price to rental rates is high  price equity ratios in uh the stock  market are high  but um i don't think we have the same  dangers that um we had in the run-up to  the 2008  uh financial crisis for example in the  housing market credit quality is  excellent we don't have the kind of  mortgage practices that we saw in the  run-up to that crisis but the outlook is  very uncertain the dangers at the global  level are high  i do worry about commodity prices um i  am worried about spillovers from russia  and ukraine that can have adverse  impacts not just on the u.s that is  strongly positioned but  on europe on emerging markets  that you know  could have spillovers to the united  states that's that's where my concerns  are centered  um your you  labor labor economics i think is your is  your specialty as an academic one of the  things we've talked a lot about at our  event  uh in the last 24 hours is how  volatile the workplace is uh how many  times will people be back two days will  they ever be back five days  how many people will go remote how many  people will  move away and never go back to a  headquarters what will happen with  office space  it's it's obviously an incredibly  dramatic and uncertain time  right now  and up for reinvention so when you think  about those aspects of the labor market  and the health of the economy  what are you seeing what are you  watching  to try to understand  changes in behavior  and think about what might be temporary  what might be permanent  and to understand what's going on  well i i agree with you i think that the  pandemic really provoked very  significant structural  changes in the workplace and some of  that is going to prove to be  permanent  um  you know some of this i think is really  healthy  um innovation in how we work how we  interact um  the fact that we can be doing what we're  now doing over  uh zoom  uh  really cheap really changes the way  people live and work and i do think  that's a healthy development  clearly people are changing jobs they  we saw  massive amounts of job shifting across  sectors um  during you know during the pandemic and  in its aftermath and  so there's a lot of structural change  taking place  overall in the labor market what i see  is a very healthy  labor market where people especially at  the lower end in lower wage jobs are  seeing very significant increases in  their wages which is  been a long time coming and uh people  are moving to opportunities into kinds  of work um quitting is it um record  highs that were ever seen in the  statistics and um part of that is a good  healthy  reallocation of people um across jobs  and sectors where uh there are better  matches and they can achieve their  potential  um i'm going to go to the audience in a  minute i know uh there are people with  questions i had a list of policy things  i wanted to get to but i'm going to ask  you one of those which is  um one of your big initiatives has been  the global minimum tax it was going to  be part of  buildback batter legislation which  now seems uh well not very likely i  guess is a fair way to put it in poland  it's uh its face resistance is the  global minimum tax going to  make it or is it is it not going to to  ever get to be enacted  well i very much hope that it will be  enacted  i  feel it's an incredibly important global  initiative that is in the interests of  the united states and  countries throughout the world and what  we've seen over decades is a race to the  bottom in terms of corporate tax rates  one country  lowering tax rates to attract businesses  and others  responding and i think putting an end to  that unhealthy  competition  so that  all countries have some ability to raise  revenues  create create a level playing field for  businesses globally  um but raise the revenue that we need to  invest in our economy in infrastructure  and investment in people  to make our economy productive this is a  very healthy shift i think if any  reconciliation package is passed in the  coming months  certainly democrats  i believe unanimously favorite uh the  provisions that would be necessary in  the united states for us to be in  compliance with the global minimum tax  they were included in the house passed  package  so  if any reconciliation package is passed  i expect us to come into compliance  i think the eu is very close to adopting  the global minimum tax uh you mentioned  poland a poland was a holdout i think  poland has some issues with the eu  frankly i don't think that they largely  pertain to the global minimum tax itself  but rather other issues um between  poland and the eu so  um i i really expect the eu to  um pass this into law uh this spring and  i think that would be a good example to  the united states to show a very  significant um piece of the economic  activity uh in the world has come into  compliance and it sets a good example  for us  so you have real hopes it sounds like  that reconciliation is going to  maybe get this done if nothing else  comes along so it's very much alive for  you it sounds like  yes i mean i don't know what will happen  with reconciliation but um i remain  hopeful that some  reconciliation a package will be passed  and i believe that this will be a  component  on the revenue raising side if any  package becomes law and you know i i  think this is an important initiative  and i would love to see bipartisan  support for it i think it's broadly in  the interest of the united states and  not partisan  do we have questions out here anybody if  we could throw up the lights for a  second anybody have any questions for uh  secretary ellen  uh  james  can i ask about the  future for the dollar and um in  particular whether you're  whether you see any limit to how much  the  treasury you oversee  can take advantage of having the global  reserve currency  um to enforce foreign policy sanctions  um most recently uh confiscating  russia's reserves obviously is the the  key for the question here is there some  limit you you can't go beyond before  countries stop using  the dollar as a safe store of value  particularly countries that worry they  might fall on the wrong side of america  at some point  well  i mean certainly the fact that the  dollar is used so widely as a reserve  currency  does mean that the u.s has the ability  to impose sanctions  that can take a severe toll  that ability needs to be used sparingly  wisely  and  when it comes to sanctions as severe as  those we've imposed on russia  the hurdle i think for imposing such  sanctions needs to be high  um  you know it  we have only rarely i believe in the  case of iran and north korea um impose  sanctions on a country's central bank  and the decision to do that to russia is  not one that was undertaken lightly  i would point out that  there it involved a very broad coalition  of countries that felt that it was  warranted that is a rare situation to  prevail  but we had the entire g7 and other  countries even including switzerland  that felt that this was an appropriate  sanction  so the bar is not low  and i think it should be it should be  high  but i think we value we value very much  and gain from having the dollar used as  a reserve currency  there are very good fundamental reasons  based on the strength of the us economy  the stability of  and  of economic management the liquidity of  our markets the safety of the dollar our  rule rule of law and institutions  that make the dollar  a  an important reserve currency i don't  see any other currency at this point  that could begin to rival that  and madam secretary i know we're short  but i'm going to take prerogative this  is the last question i promise and thank  you again for your time but since sure  hadn't really gotten to russia and  you've been running you've been the  point person for russia sanctions let me  ask you two russia related questions to  end  one is we've imposed various rounds of  sanctions  on russia how much more potentially  could there be and and how much more  even beyond what it is severe could it  get  and and by the same token  when you think ahead to a time beyond  the war are you yet thinking in any way  about  whether and how  it might even be possible to  reforge some economic ties  with russia down the road or are we on a  path to where  even after the war russia is going to be  isolated for for the foreseeable future  how are you thinking about that  well let me just say  we're we're certainly are looking at  further measures that we can take  um to respond to russia's aggression  against ukraine and we're in constant  discussions with our partners about uh  further sanctioned packages i don't want  to preview specifics but um there are  certainly additional actions that we can  take and probably would take if if  russia continues this war against  ukraine  um  although i do believe our sanctions have  been quite effective  and  have really made a huge difference  to the economic outlook both long-term  and short-term in russia inflation is  very high the russian economy is clearly  going into recession the russians have  admitted that and it's taken an enormous  toll in terms of foreign investment and  access to goods that russia needs  to compete uh over the long term i think  it's really damaged  russia but yes there's a path to  de-escalation of sanctions and um if  you know if there's a truce that's  risked or a peace agreement um it would  be certainly appropriate to  consider a path to de-escalation and i  don't have anything specific to give you  on that but certainly that needs and is  part of the thinking  okay well treasury secretary jenny  yellen thank you very much for taking  this time with us today at such an  eventful moment we really appreciate uh  your time so thank you very much  [Applause]  [Music]  [Applause]
 

